  <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
  <div class="draft" role="note" aria-label="Draft notice">
    <p><strong>DRAFT. Pending lawyer review under India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023.</strong></p>
    <p>This is a placeholder and is not legally reviewed. It must not be relied on until this notice is removed.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who we are</h2>
  <p>Plate &amp; Bar is an app that is still in development and not yet released. It is run by [operator name]. Contact: [privacy contact email].</p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>The app shows you this before you start:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your data stays on this device. If you sign in, it is also stored on our own server so it syncs between your devices.</li>
    <li>Progress photos, cycle logs and lab reports stay only on this device.</li>
    <li>AI features are off by default and need their own opt-in. Your progress photos are never sent anywhere. The AI photo features, when switched on, send only the picture you choose for that one request.</li>
    <li>You can download or delete everything at any time.</li>
    <li>Withdrawing your consent means deleting your account.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What stays on your phone</h2>
  <p>Progress photos, cycle data and lab results stay on your device and are never uploaded. The app works without an account. Your setup answers, health-check answers, body measurements, meals, workouts and settings are saved on your device first.</p>
  <p>The cycle log is optional and off until you turn it on. It is kept in its own place on your device, is never synced, never sent to our server and never sent to an AI service, and has no field in our server's records. It is included in the copy you download from your device (not in the copy from our server), and "Delete everything" and "Turn off and delete" in the log itself remove it. It is also removed from the device when another account signs in on it and on "Discard and sign out", because the log belongs to the account that is cleared.</p>

  <h2>What we store on our server</h2>
  <p>Before you sign in: if you ask for an email sign-in code, the server stores your email address, a hashed copy of the code and a record of failed code attempts, which it uses to limit guessing.</p>
  <p>When you sign in, the server creates your account and your sign-in sessions. </p>
  <p>Syncing your records is separate. You give consent on the "Before you start" screen, or with "Agree and sync" when you sign in, and nothing is uploaded until you do. After that, the server stores:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>profile and targets, and settings;</li>
    <li>food, water and workout logs, and day notes;</li>
    <li>weights and body measurements;</li>
    <li>your own foods, recipes and kitchen tests;</li>
    <li>exclusions and swaps;</li>
    <li>your consent record.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The server also keeps a conflict log. If two devices change the same record, it keeps a copy of the version that lost so that nothing is silently lost. The server keeps your newest 1,000 conflict entries and drops entries older than 90 days. This clean-up runs when a later conflict is logged, so until then an entry can be older than 90 days. The log is a safety net for recovering a lost edit, not a full history. If you use AI features, it keeps a daily counter of your AI requests. Separately, it keeps one anonymous total of AI tokens used per month (the month and the token count only, with no user id, day or feature) to enforce a shared monthly AI budget. It cannot be linked to any account.</p>

  <h2>Who else handles your data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Email delivery</strong>: if you sign in with an email code, your email address and the code go through our email provider, [SMTP provider].</li>
    <li><strong>Hosting</strong>: our server runs at [hosting provider].</li>
    <li><strong>Backups</strong>: [backup arrangement: where nightly database backups and the erasure ledger copy are kept, and whether they are encrypted].</li>
    <li><strong>Error tracking</strong>: when crash reporting is on, reports go to [error-tracking service host] (see "Crash and error reports").</li>
    <li><strong>AI service</strong>: AI features are off by default and you switch them on yourself; the app works without them. When you use one, the app sends the items below to our server, which passes them to [AI provider]. It never sends your name, email, progress photos, cycle or lab data.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>What each AI feature sends:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Describe a meal: the text you type.</li>
    <li>Ask why: the card name and the question you type.</li>
    <li>Suggest muscles: when you add a custom exercise with AI on, the name you typed for it, so the AI can suggest its muscles and other tags.</li>
    <li>Weekly summary: sessions this week and sessions planned.</li>
    <li>Weekly summary: days with food logged, average calories and average protein.</li>
    <li>Weekly summary: days at the protein target (90% or more).</li>
    <li>Weekly summary: average weight this week and last week.</li>
    <li>Weekly summary: the lifts that improved (with the percent gain over their previous best) and the lifts that stalled. In this summary only, custom exercise names are replaced with "custom exercise".</li>
    <li>Weekly summary: your calorie and protein targets, your goal and your estimated daily burn.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When the AI image features are switched on (they are off by default), three more features can send a picture: a meal photo, a photo of a recipe (a recipe pasted as text sends no picture), and a few still frames from a form-check video. Our server re-encodes each image as a new picture, which removes its hidden data (such as location and time), and passes only that, with your text, to [AI provider]. The images are never stored or logged, in files, the database, a cache or reports, and are kept in memory only while the request runs. The video itself never leaves your phone.</p>
  <p>Our server does not save what you send or get back; for the text features it may keep an answer in memory, for you only, for up to 24 hours. Image calls are never cached.</p>
  <p>The app also fetches shared reference content (foods, exercises, cards and similar) from our server. This content is the same for everyone and contains none of your data.</p>

  <h2>Device features and permissions</h2>
  <p>The app (on the web, your browser) asks for these permissions only when you turn on the feature that needs them:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Step counter</strong> (physical activity permission, off by default): the app reads your phone's step sensor on the device. The count is made on your phone, and the day's total then syncs like steps you type yourself, when you have agreed to sync. Nothing else from the sensor leaves the phone.</li>
    <li><strong>Reminders</strong> (notifications permission): reminders are local notifications scheduled on your phone. We do not run a push notification service, and no reminder text or token is sent to us or to anyone else.</li>
    <li><strong>Camera</strong>: for barcode scanning and live pose tracking, camera frames never leave your phone and no video or picture is saved. The camera is also used to take progress photos, which stay encrypted on this device and are never sent, and, when the AI image features are on, to take the meal photo described above, which is sent as described there. When you scan a barcode, only its digits are sent to our server, which asks Open Food Facts for that one product. Our server keeps the answer in a per-barcode cache for 30 days (24 hours when the product is not found) with no user id, and our logs redact the code. If you save a scanned food, its barcode is kept in your own synced food list. The Open Food Facts data is under its own licence, see <a href="/licenses/">licences</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Live pose tracking</strong> (off by default): the camera frames are analysed on your device only, and only the rep count, if you choose to use it, goes into that set of your workout log. Depth is never stored.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Crash and error reports</h2>
  <p>Crash and error reporting is built in but stays off until we switch it on for a release. When it is on, the app and our server send error reports to [error-tracking service host], an error-tracking service we run ourselves on [hosting provider] that accepts Sentry-compatible reports. We use reports only to find and fix bugs.</p>
  <p>From the app, a report contains:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the type of error and the stack frames (function name, file name without any query, line number);</li>
    <li>device model, manufacturer and brand, operating system and app version, plus the release and environment names;</li>
    <li>your language and time zone (which can hint at your region), and technical device details such as memory and storage size and free space, screen size, density and orientation, whether the device is rooted, the app identifier, build and start time, and the list of libraries in the app;</li>
    <li>a random install id kept on your device. It is not tied to your account, and on Android it is different if you reinstall;</li>
    <li>the type, category, level and time of earlier app events, with no message or content.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The error-reporting library also sends a short session report each time the app is used, which holds the start time, how long the session lasted, whether it ended in a crash, the app release and the same install id. This is sent even when nothing went wrong.</p>
  <p>Crashes outside the app's JavaScript (native crashes, such as uncaught Java or Kotlin exceptions, crashes in the app's own native libraries and crashes in Android system code) are sent by the library directly and are not filtered by the app's own scrubbing. They can include the system's error message, the list of running threads and the library's full device details, such as battery level.</p>
  <p>From our server, a report contains the error type, the stack trace, the request method and path (ids in the path are masked), the app or browser's user-agent string, and one hashed account id that does not reveal your email, name or IP address.</p>
  <p>Reports from the app's own code and from our server never contain food, weight, health answers, messages, email addresses, sign-in tokens or photos, and the text of the error is blanked because it can echo what you typed. Request bodies, cookies and most headers are removed before such a report leaves the app or the server. Native Android crashes are the exception described above. No advertising or screenshots are sent, and nothing else about how you use the app is sent apart from the session report above.</p>
  <p>The Android app declares the "network state" permission (ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE). The error-reporting library uses it to tell whether the phone is online when it sends a report.</p>

  <h2>Server logs</h2>
  <p>Our server logs never contain food, weight, health answers or tokens. They record an opaque internal user identifier with operational events such as sign-in, sync, deletion, export and AI requests (and, for AI, which feature, the outcome and how long it took).</p>
  <p>The web servers in front of our API and the web app also keep an access log, with one line per request: the time, the method, the path without the query string (the part after "?"), the status, the request and response sizes, how long it took, the host name, technical connection details (the HTTP and TLS versions, the cipher and the port number your device used) and your IP address with its end masked (IPv4 to /24, IPv6 to /48), so it points to a block of addresses rather than one device. It has no user id and holds no request or response headers (so no cookies, sign-in tokens or user-agent) and no request bodies. When these servers log an error about a request (for example when the API cannot be reached), that line is filtered the same way.</p>

  <h2>Retention</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your account and records stay until you delete your account. The conflict log is bounded as described above.</li>
    <li>The anonymous monthly AI token total has no user id and stays when an account is deleted, so deleting an account does not give its AI use back to the shared budget.</li>
    <li>Backups: [planned policy to confirm: nightly backups kept for 30 days; after you delete your account, copies in backups are not edited and disappear when those backups expire, at most 30 days later].</li>
    <li>Erasure ledger: when an account is deleted, the server records only the account's opaque user id and the time of deletion, with nothing else about you, so that restoring a backup cannot bring the account back. A copy is kept with the backups. In both places, each entry is kept for about 45 days (at most 46), then deleted.</li>
    <li>Crash and error reports: kept for [error report retention period], then deleted. A report holds no health data and only an opaque id, so deleting your account does not remove it. [Lawyer review: the hashed account id in server reports is a pseudonym that we can recompute while your account exists, so confirm whether reports must be deleted with the account.]</li>
    <li>Barcode lookup cache: a product fact is kept for 30 days (24 hours when not found), then expires. It holds the barcode and the product facts only, with no user id.</li>
    <li>Access logs of the web servers: kept for at most about 15 days, then deleted.</li>
    <li>Server logs record opaque user ids and are kept for a bounded period. Retention: the API's own logs and the web server's error lines are kept for at most 14 days, and for less if the logs reach about 200 MB on the server.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your choices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Download your data</strong>: in the app, "Your data" lets you download a copy made on your device, and, when signed in, a copy from our server. The server copy contains your account record, all your synced records and settings, and the conflict log. The conflict log is bounded (the newest 1,000 entries, older than 90 days dropped), so earlier conflicts may be absent. It does not contain sign-in sessions, one-time codes or AI usage counters.</li>
    <li><strong>Restore from a file</strong>: in the app, "Your data" can restore a copy made on your device (not the copy from our server). The app reads and checks the file on your device, and the file is not uploaded for the restore. It refuses a file that does not pass the checks, and it shows what would change and asks you to confirm before it changes anything. Restoring never wipes everything and loads the file. It merges record by record: a newer record in the file replaces the one on your device, a record deleted in the file is deleted on your device (including the photo of a kitchen test), and a newer record on your device is kept. A record you changed on this device and that has not synced yet is always kept as it is. Your consents are never restored from a file. If the file came from a different account, it only adds records your device does not have. When you are signed in, these changes sync like any other edit, including deletions, and records from a different account's file sync to the account on this device. The cycle log and lab reports in the file are restored on this device only and are never synced: the cycle log only when this device has none, and lab reports only when they are missing. The file can turn the lab hold on but never off. Progress photos are not in the file, so they are not restored. The app deletes its temporary copy of the file after reading it.</li>
    <li><strong>Withdraw consent</strong>: withdrawing your consent means deleting your account, and you can delete your account at any time from "Your data" in the app.</li>
    <li><strong>Delete your data</strong>: in the app, "Delete everything" deletes your account and its server data, then clears the device. See <a href="/delete-account/">how to delete your account</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>AI features</strong> can be switched off at any time in the app.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="your-rights">Your rights under the DPDP Act, 2023</h2>
  <p>Under India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 you are a data principal and have these rights over your personal data:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Access</strong>: ask for a summary of the personal data we hold about you and how we use it, including who we have shared it with (see "Who else handles your data"). In the app, "Your data" downloads a copy made on your device, and that works signed out and offline; when signed in it can also download a copy from our server.</li>
    <li><strong>Correction and updating</strong>: ask us to correct wrong data or complete and update it. You can edit your own records in the app, and edits sync to our server.</li>
    <li><strong>Erasure</strong>: ask for your personal data to be erased. In the app, "Delete everything" does this at any time. See <a href="/delete-account/">how to delete your account</a> for what is and is not deleted.</li>
    <li><strong>Grievance redressal</strong>: complain to us about how we handle your data or your rights. Contact the grievance officer below first.</li>
    <li><strong>Nominate</strong>: name another person who can exercise your rights for you if you die or become unable to act. [Nomination process, to be confirmed with legal review.]</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To use a right that the app does not cover, email [privacy contact email]. We may need to check it is really you first. [Response time we commit to, for example the number of days, to be confirmed with legal review.]</p>

  <h2>Grievance officer</h2>
  <p>Name: [grievance officer name]<br />Email: [grievance officer email]<br />Postal address: [grievance officer address]</p>
  <p>If we do not resolve your complaint within [grievance response period], you may complain to the Data Protection Board of India, as the Act provides. [Confirm the Board's current process and contact with legal review.]</p>

  <h2>Changes and the rest</h2>
  <p>Any other legal terms, and the date of this policy, will be added with legal review. See also the <a href="/terms/">terms of use</a>.</p>
